WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.474
Imagine walking into a review with a dashboard redesign.

00:00:04.474 --> 00:00:09.445
You have evidence that people struggle with the current screen.

00:00:09.445 --> 00:00:11.931
You have a proposed fix.

00:00:11.931 --> 00:00:23.861
You put up the first slide and someone asks, “Is this the best use of engineering time right now?” That is a reasonable question.

00:00:23.861 --> 00:00:29.827
It may feel like a rejection, but it is also a clue.

00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:37.780
The person across the table is being asked to trade one piece of work for another.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:44.243
A pile of research about the screen does not yet explain the trade.

00:00:44.243 --> 00:00:50.208
Chapter Four of Accelerated tells this as the dashboard that almost died.

00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:56.173
The first pitch described the confusing dashboard and asked for a redesign.

00:00:56.173 --> 00:01:05.121
The later pitch put options on the table, including a smaller change, a larger bet, and a delay.

00:01:05.121 --> 00:01:09.595
The book reports a decision after the frame changed.

00:01:09.595 --> 00:01:18.046
In this workshop we use the case to study the question, not to promise the same result.

00:01:18.046 --> 00:01:23.514
What would an owner need to believe before moving engineering time?

00:01:23.514 --> 00:01:26.000
That is where discovery starts.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.659
Open the priority page you made in Lesson Two.

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:36.871
You chose one path and wrote an assumption that could overturn it.

00:01:36.871 --> 00:01:41.529
Now write the decision owner’s question in their words.

00:01:41.529 --> 00:02:14.141
For the dashboard, it might be, “What are we giving up to do this, and what would improve if we did?” For our fictional membership service, it might be, “Will a guided first task help people begin, or will it add another step?” That is more useful than “Do users like the idea?” The answer you believe today goes on the next line.

00:02:14.141 --> 00:02:15.694
Label it provisional.

00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:21.388
Under that, write the strongest objection a thoughtful colleague could raise.

00:02:21.388 --> 00:02:27.082
The objection is not a nuisance to remove from the slide.

00:02:27.082 --> 00:02:31.224
It tells you what evidence the decision needs.

00:02:31.224 --> 00:02:40.541
The chapter argues that insight can land flat when its frame ignores the risk the other person carries.

00:02:40.541 --> 00:02:45.200
So we work backward: decision, likely answer, objection, evidence.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:50.376
Only then do we choose what to observe or ask.

00:02:50.376 --> 00:02:54.000
The order keeps research attached to action.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.605
A single polished idea invites a yes or no.

00:02:58.605 --> 00:03:02.186
Chapter Four calls this the binary trap.

00:03:02.186 --> 00:03:13.442
The dashboard case becomes easier to discuss when the team can compare a quick simplification, a deeper rebuild, and a deliberate delay.

00:03:13.442 --> 00:03:20.093
Each path has a different cost and a different thing it could teach.

00:03:20.093 --> 00:03:23.674
Your priority file already has two alternatives.

00:03:23.674 --> 00:03:28.791
Bring them forward rather than hiding them after you choose.

00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:34.930
For the membership example, the welcome email could be the light change.

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:39.023
The guided task is the small behavior test.

00:03:39.023 --> 00:03:42.605
The staff dashboard is a visibility investment.

00:03:42.605 --> 00:03:47.209
None of those labels is a fact about customers.

00:03:47.209 --> 00:03:51.302
They are ways to make the trade visible.

00:03:51.302 --> 00:04:03.070
If the objection is “We are solving the wrong part of onboarding,” the evidence should tell you where a person stops and why.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:11.767
If the objection is “We cannot support another workflow,” the evidence may need to include staff effort.

00:04:11.767 --> 00:04:15.860
A stronger frame does not mean louder language.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:22.000
It means the choice is clear enough for another person to challenge.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:27.696
Suppose you can ask only one more question before the review.

00:04:27.696 --> 00:04:30.286
What would change your mind?

00:04:30.286 --> 00:04:32.875
That question keeps discovery honest.

00:04:32.875 --> 00:04:45.821
If you would make the same recommendation no matter what the answer is, you may be collecting support for a story rather than testing it.

00:04:45.821 --> 00:04:52.036
The book suggests pressure-testing a pitch from different angles and anticipating objections.

00:04:52.036 --> 00:04:59.286
You can do that with a safe, fictional brief before anyone sees the recommendation.

00:04:59.286 --> 00:05:06.018
Ask what a skeptical finance lead, an operator, or a customer might question.

00:05:06.018 --> 00:05:11.714
Then compare those possible objections with what real evidence is available.

00:05:11.714 --> 00:05:16.375
An AI tool can help you rehearse the questions.

00:05:16.375 --> 00:05:21.554
It cannot turn a simulated objection into a customer interview.

00:05:21.554 --> 00:05:26.214
On our card, write one evidence gap that matters.

00:05:26.214 --> 00:05:38.643
Maybe we do not know whether people abandon the first step because they cannot find it, cannot understand it, or do not value it.

00:05:38.643 --> 00:05:40.714
Those are different problems.

00:05:40.714 --> 00:05:49.000
We should not build a guided path for all three without learning which one we have.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.618
Let's fill the rest of the card.

00:05:52.618 --> 00:05:57.787
The decision is whether to test a guided first task.

00:05:57.787 --> 00:06:05.539
Our current answer is yes, because the team suspects people need a clearer starting point.

00:06:05.539 --> 00:06:14.843
The strongest objection is that the first step may already be clear; maybe the real issue comes later.

00:06:14.843 --> 00:06:25.697
The missing evidence is an observation of where people hesitate and a short question about what they expected to happen next.

00:06:25.697 --> 00:06:33.449
A safe test could use a prototype and willing participants under an approved research process.

00:06:33.449 --> 00:06:40.169
We would record what they did, what they said, and what remains uncertain.

00:06:40.169 --> 00:06:42.236
That last field matters.

00:06:42.236 --> 00:06:49.989
A few observations can direct the next question; they do not establish a population-wide result.

00:06:49.989 --> 00:06:56.708
If you have no approved research access, keep the card as a plan.

00:06:56.708 --> 00:07:02.393
Do not manufacture quotes or silently substitute simulated people for participants.

00:07:02.393 --> 00:07:11.180
The dashboard case in the book helps us see how a named trade can make evidence relevant.

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:21.000
Our exercise turns that lesson into an action you can take without pretending that the answer is already known.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.126
Pause and make your own card.

00:07:24.126 --> 00:07:27.253
Write the decision in a sentence.

00:07:27.253 --> 00:07:30.379
Write the answer you currently believe.

00:07:30.379 --> 00:07:32.984
Write the strongest fair objection.

00:07:32.984 --> 00:07:42.363
Then write one piece of evidence that could change the decision and a safe way to seek it.

00:07:42.363 --> 00:07:48.095
If your evidence is already available, record its source and date.

00:07:48.095 --> 00:07:51.221
If it is missing, say missing.

00:07:51.221 --> 00:07:57.995
Your file should now show a line from priority choice to evidence gap.

00:07:57.995 --> 00:08:03.205
That line is the point of these first three lessons.

00:08:03.205 --> 00:08:10.500
A good question can save you from a month of defending the wrong artifact.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:15.711
When you resume, look at the person behind the evidence.

00:08:15.711 --> 00:08:24.568
We tend to summarize that person as a persona and leave the slide untouched for a year.

00:08:24.568 --> 00:08:33.947
In the next lesson we will make the customer situation more specific and the persona easier to revise.

00:08:33.947 --> 00:08:40.721
You do not have to win the room with a perfect story today.

00:08:40.721 --> 00:08:52.184
You need a decision that another person can understand, an objection you have taken seriously, and evidence you would actually listen to.

00:08:52.184 --> 00:08:56.353
Keep the card attached to your priority page.

00:08:56.353 --> 00:09:00.000
We are going to use it again.
